        "title": "CLI Login Auth Code Flow",
        "summary": "Implements one-time auth code generation and validation for CLI login via `login-code-request` and `login-status-request` WebSocket actions. Backend verifies hash using `NEXTAUTH_SECRET` and links `fingerprintId` to user sessions. Adds `fingerprintId` and `fingerprintHash` columns to `session` table with new migration. Scope includes `common/src/actions.ts`, `backend/src/websockets/websocket-action.ts`, and `common/src/db/migrations`.",
        "significance": "major",
        "at": "2026-09-15T18:58:59.651Z"
      },
      "eli5": {
        "text": "When you log into the command-line tool on a new computer, it now shows a one-time code on a screen that you visit in your web browser to prove it's really you, then the browser opens and you can finish signing in through your normal Google or GitHub flow. That code is generated by the backend, checked against a secret value known only to the system, and tied to your specific device using a fingerprint-like identifier so your session on that machine is linked to your account. If you use the command-line tool to sign in, you'll see a link appear that opens automatically in your web browser before redirecting you through OAuth. This feature is live today for anyone signing in through the CLI, replacing the previous method that was having environment configuration problems.",
        "model": "vyce/deepseek-v4.1",
